<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohawk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanienʼkehá꞉ka, Keepers of the Eastern Door, Maqua, Agnier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mohawk, in their own language Kanienʼkehá꞉ka, People of the Flint Place, are an Indigenous people of the Northeastern Woodlands and the easternmost nation of the Haudenosaunee, or Iroquois Confederacy, known as the Keepers of the Eastern Door. Their homeland centred on the Mohawk Valley of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Their society rests on three matrilineal clans, Bear, Turtle and Wolf, led by women; children belong to their mother's clan, and Clan Mothers nominate the hereditary chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> reduced them from 7,740 to 2,830 in 1635. They sided with the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> and today live in communities in New York, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohawk-people.jpg" alt="Mohawk" width="330" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Stuart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Brant_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Together they persuaded the <b>Mohawk</b>, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca to accept the Great Law of Peace, the Gayanashagowa, and finally won over the feared Onondaga chief Tadodaho, whose name became the title of the league’s presiding chief.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Champlain’s fight against the <b>Mohawk</b> near Lake Champlain in 1609 began a long hostility with the Iroquois Confederacy, whose raids in the 1650s threatened the colony’s survival.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their first partners were the Algonquins, and they depended on Native nations to capture, skin and deliver pelts, above all beaver; after the <b>Mohawks</b> of the Iroquois Confederacy conquered the Mahicans in 1628, they held a near-monopoly of the trade through the Adirondacks and the Mohawk Valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century such men led their nations in the colonial wars: the old <b>Mohawk</b> war chief Peter Hendricks was killed at Lake George in 1755, and Joseph Brant raised forces for the Crown in the Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Red River Valley&quot; is a folk song and cowboy standard of uncertain origin, sung under many names, among them &quot;Cowboy Love Song&quot;, &quot;Bright Sherman Valley&quot;, &quot;In the Bright <b>Mohawk</b> Valley&quot; and &quot;Bright Little Valley&quot;, depending on where it was sung.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-river-valley">Red River Valley</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonists, some disguised as <b>Mohawk</b> Indians, boarded three ships in Boston Harbor and threw 342 chests of East India Company tea into the water.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-16">16 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
